[{:db/id -1
  :adr/id "adr-2609071000-the-frame-cannot-be-wider-than-the-terminal"
  :adr/title "The frame cannot be wider than the terminal, and the width is measured"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-09-07"
  :adr/related "adr-2609062800-the-input-area-is-an-editor-not-a-readline"
  :adr/body "## The report

2026-09-06, from the operator, against a screenshot: キーボードカーソルが動かない,
入力すると入力エリアが消えてしまう. And a second screenshot of another agent CLI:
show what is happening while a turn runs.

## What was actually wrong

The frame is redrawn by moving the cursor up by the number of rows it occupies,
clearing to the end of the screen, and printing it again. That arithmetic is
only true while every row FITS the terminal.

Every row is built to fit -- but \"fits\" was this program's opinion of how wide
a glyph is, and UAX#11 does not settle that. U+276F (the prompt), U+25B6 (the
status bar), U+00B7 (every separator) and all of box drawing (both rules) are
East Asian Ambiguous: one column in a Latin locale, two in a CJK one. The
program assumed one.

When the opinion is short by even one column the row wraps, the block occupies
more terminal rows than were counted, the next erase moves up too few, and the
input area walks down the screen leaving copies of itself while the caret lands
somewhere it was not put. That is what カーソルが動かない and
入力エリアが消えてしまう are, from outside.

Reproduced 2026-09-07 by making every row twelve columns too wide on a pty of
known size: stacked, drifting copies of the input area, exactly as reported.
Nothing else about the build changed.

## Three fixes, in order of how much they are relied on

**1. Auto-wrap off while the block is drawn** (`ESC[?7l` … `ESC[?7h`). The
terminal clips a too-long row instead of wrapping it, so the block is always
exactly the number of rows that were printed, whatever anyone thinks a glyph
is worth. Verified with the same twelve-column error: frame intact. Verified in
the other direction by leaving wrap on with the same error: the reported
failure, reproduced.

This is the one that must hold. The two below make the screen right; this one
makes it impossible for the screen to be broken.

**2. The ambiguous width is measured, not assumed.** At startup, on a terminal,
the program prints U+276F at a known column, asks where the cursor ended up
with `ESC[6n`, and subtracts. A terminal that does not answer within 300 ms
leaves the default of 1 standing AND says so on stderr -- an unanswered query
must not read as an answer.

`itonami-text/display-width` consults that number for the ambiguous ranges. Only
1 and 2 are accepted as answers.

**3. `text/rule`.** Writing `(.repeat \"─\" n)` was wrong by construction: U+2500
is itself Ambiguous, so on a terminal that draws it in two columns the rule came
out twice the width of the frame it was drawing. The editor's own width check
caught this the first time it ran against a measured width of 2 -- which is the
argument for measuring rather than for picking a number.

Also, per redraw: the cursor is hidden, and each row is cleared with `ESC[2K`
rather than trusting one `ESC[0J` to have reached every column the last frame
used.

## The line that runs under a turn

`editor/progress-line`: a spinner, the phase the RUN reported, how long it has
been going, tokens once any have been counted, and `esc で中断` while that is
true. It owns one terminal row; everything else that writes during a run takes
it off first, and from the first `assistant.delta` it does not come back --
it would interleave with the text it was waiting for.

There is no percentage and no bar. The server sends phases, not progress, and a
bar filling on a timer would be the one thing on that screen that nothing
measured. The test asserts their absence.

The clock is not restarted when a tool log takes the row: the run has been
going the whole time, and a timer that reset would report a wait that never
happened.

Measured against the live resident: `✳ working… (0s · esc で中断)` through
`✵ model… (30s · esc で中断)`, then gone, with no residue on the screen the
answer lands on.

## What this cost, and what it was worth

A `git checkout bin/itonami` meant to undo an experiment's hack discarded an
hour of uncommitted work in the same file. Reapplied, then committed before the
next experiment. The lesson is the ordinary one and it is written here because
it happened: an experiment that edits a working file wants its own copy, not a
checkout to undo it.
"}]
